1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,018
When the moon went dark
over ancient Assyria,

2
00:00:03,018 --> 00:00:05,918
the king's scholars had
a way to save his life.

3
00:00:05,932 --> 00:00:09,605
They put another man on his throne
— and when the danger had passed,

4
00:00:09,605 --> 00:00:12,385
they let that man die in the king's place.

5
00:00:12,431 --> 00:00:15,646
The scholars preferred a man
whose death would trouble no one:

6
00:00:15,646 --> 00:00:18,651
a condemned criminal,
a prisoner, a laborer.

7
00:00:18,651 --> 00:00:23,423
But in 671 BCE, the substitute
was someone who mattered.

8
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:28,127
Damqi was the son of a high temple
official in the Babylonian city of Akkad.

9
00:00:28,127 --> 00:00:32,399
Damqi, "who had ruled Assyria,
Babylonia and all the countries,

10
00:00:32,504 --> 00:00:35,612
died with his queen," as
a substitute for the king.

11
00:00:35,612 --> 00:00:41,044
"He and his queen were decorated, treated,
displayed, buried and wailed over."

12
00:00:41,044 --> 00:00:43,151
Then the report adds something else.

13
00:00:43,151 --> 00:00:48,677
Before the ritual, a prophetess had told
Damqi: "You will take over the kingship!"

14
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:50,040
She was right.
